 "log": [
  "Evidence has 4 meshes (glassDish, olives, glassCover, goldLeaf); material names were used as handed-over evidence (the plan 'basis' vocabulary has no 'material' entry, so a first check refused it; basis only allows position/size/colour/shape).",
  "Mesh 0 islands 0 and 1 share the same size and centre (two halves of one thin plate, texture u split at 0.5): evidence does not separate them, so merged into one whole part Glass_Dish. Same for gold leaf islands 0/1 (same footprint as the dish, near-identical size).",
  "Glass_Dish and Glass_Cover are named from the material names only (glass + dish/cover); size/at only supports 'wide flat disc' and 'single 0.34x0.26x0.35 object'. I did not claim a dome/bowl shape or what the cover sits on.",
  "Mesh 1 has 48 identical-size olive islands. Only two colour families are cleanly separable (dark ~ r 60-76 vs green/yellow ~ r 130-165); within each there are sub-shades (e.g. 163,147,61 brighter vs 132,118,49 greener; 61,32,29 vs 76,40,36) but I could not tell whether these are different olive kinds or texture variation, so I did not split them further.",
  "Olive names are descriptive: colour + height (Raised if at-y>0.4) + 5-band column letter on x + 8-band row letter on z. Direction of x/z (left/right, front/back) is not given by the evidence so I used neutral letters; _Altx suffixes only disambiguate islands falling in the same cell. These are not authored names; the unique-name rule has no good answer for 48 identical things without index-like codes.",
  "The brief says no numbered placeholders; position-cell codes are effectively indices, I judged them acceptable because they carry position evidence.",
  "Gold leaf islands 2-7 are zero-height (size y 0.000) tiny flat pieces at at-y 1.00 (top of the mesh), toward the high-z edge; I merged them as 'Flecks' since size/colour does not distinguish them. Could equally be a flat decal/marking or separate pieces; unresolved.",
  "Writing a 48-part plan by hand is very verbose; had to generate names with a helper script (reading only my own evidence)."
